WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.733 --> 00:00:05.762
A Dockerfile ends with a line that builds nothing.

2
00:00:05.762 --> 00:00:08.912
It only says what the container runs when it starts.

3
00:00:09.867 --> 00:00:12.945
Here are two ways to write that line, for the same app.

4
00:00:13.133 --> 00:00:15.956
The lesson timed docker stop on both.

5
00:00:15.956 --> 00:00:18.779
One container stopped in 0.6 seconds.

6
00:00:18.779 --> 00:00:21.068
The other needed 10.2 seconds.

7
00:00:21.267 --> 00:00:22.672
Same app, same image.

8
00:00:22.672 --> 00:00:24.412
So why the big difference?

9
00:00:24.412 --> 00:00:26.620
Let's build it up from the start.

10
00:00:27.400 --> 00:00:29.194
First, CMD and ENTRYPOINT.

11
00:00:29.194 --> 00:00:32.161
Both say what runs when a container starts.

12
00:00:32.161 --> 00:00:34.439
Take a tiny image called greeter.

13
00:00:34.600 --> 00:00:39.019
When you type docker run greeter, Docker puts the two together.

14
00:00:39.019 --> 00:00:41.896
ENTRYPOINT gives the program: echo Hello.

15
00:00:41.896 --> 00:00:44.561
CMD gives its default argument: world.

16
00:00:44.733 --> 00:00:46.809
So it prints: Hello, world.

17
00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:50.911
Now type a name after the image: docker run greeter Ana.

18
00:00:51.067 --> 00:00:53.038
What you type replaces CMD.

19
00:00:53.038 --> 00:00:55.155
Ana takes the place of world.

20
00:00:55.333 --> 00:00:56.841
But ENTRYPOINT stays.

21
00:00:56.841 --> 00:01:00.430
Ana is added after it, so the program is the same.

22
00:01:00.430 --> 00:01:02.009
It prints: Hello, Ana.

23
00:01:02.200 --> 00:01:05.717
So CMD is a default, and ENTRYPOINT is fixed.

24
00:01:05.717 --> 00:01:09.782
Only the flag --entrypoint swaps the program itself.

25
00:01:09.782 --> 00:01:13.377
For a web app, CMD alone is the simple choice.

26
00:01:14.133 --> 00:01:16.086
Next, how you write the line.

27
00:01:16.086 --> 00:01:17.432
There are two forms.

28
00:01:17.600 --> 00:01:19.549
Exec form is a JSON list.

29
00:01:19.549 --> 00:01:22.044
Docker starts gunicorn directly.

30
00:01:22.044 --> 00:01:26.879
So your app runs as PID 1, the first process in the container.

31
00:01:27.067 --> 00:01:28.846
Shell form is plain text.

32
00:01:28.846 --> 00:01:33.544
Docker runs it through a shell: /bin/sh -c, and then your command.

33
00:01:33.733 --> 00:01:38.113
Now the shell is PID 1, and gunicorn is only its child.

34
00:01:38.267 --> 00:01:40.395
Now run docker stop on both.

35
00:01:40.395 --> 00:01:43.892
Docker sends a signal called SIGTERM to PID 1.

36
00:01:43.892 --> 00:01:46.628
It means: please finish up and exit.

37
00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:49.186
On the right, gunicorn gets it.

38
00:01:49.186 --> 00:01:54.728
It finishes its work, and exits on its own, with code 0, in 0.6 seconds.

39
00:01:54.867 --> 00:01:56.844
On the left, the shell gets it.

40
00:01:56.844 --> 00:02:00.161
But the shell does not pass SIGTERM on to its child.

41
00:02:00.161 --> 00:02:01.692
Gunicorn never hears it.

42
00:02:01.867 --> 00:02:03.001
So Docker waits.

43
00:02:03.001 --> 00:02:06.403
After 10 seconds, it gives up and sends SIGKILL.

44
00:02:07.400 --> 00:02:11.432
The container ends with code 137, after 10.2 seconds.

45
00:02:11.432 --> 00:02:19.573
In production, every deploy then waits ten extra seconds per container, and requests in flight are cut off.

46
00:02:20.467 --> 00:02:22.978
So is exec form the whole fix?

47
00:02:22.978 --> 00:02:23.815
Not quite.

48
00:02:23.815 --> 00:02:25.991
PID 1 is special in Linux.

49
00:02:26.133 --> 00:02:30.571
The kernel drops any signal that PID 1 has no handler for.

50
00:02:30.571 --> 00:02:32.867
SIGKILL is the only exception.

51
00:02:33.067 --> 00:02:38.295
A handler is code in the program that says what to do when a signal arrives.

52
00:02:38.295 --> 00:02:40.291
Gunicorn has one for SIGTERM.

53
00:02:40.467 --> 00:02:42.725
A bare Python script has none.

54
00:02:42.725 --> 00:02:47.392
So even in exec form, as PID 1, its SIGTERM is simply dropped.

55
00:02:47.533 --> 00:02:53.705
In the lesson's test, it waited out the full 10 seconds, just like sleep did on Day 17.

56
00:02:54.467 --> 00:02:55.797
So you need both.

57
00:02:55.797 --> 00:02:58.302
Exec form, so your app is PID 1.

58
00:02:58.302 --> 00:03:02.764
And an app that handles SIGTERM itself, as gunicorn does.

59
00:03:02.933 --> 00:03:06.167
ENTRYPOINT is fixed, and CMD is a default.

60
00:03:06.167 --> 00:03:11.864
Write both as a JSON list, so your app is PID 1, and it hears docker stop.
